Refineries cannot produce transportation fuels without hydrogen.

The table below summarizes the amount of hydrogen (in standard cubic feet, scf) per barrel of hydrocarbon (bbl) needed for common refinery operations. This helps give a rough estimate of hydrogen consumption. For example, a 50,000 BPD (barrel per day) gas oil hydrotreater would be expected to use about 50 MMSCFD (million scf per day) of hydrogen. [1]

| Process | SCF/BBL |
|---|---|
| **Hydrotreating** | |
| &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;VGO (Gasoil) | 800–1200 |
| &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Distillate | 300–800 |
| &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Naphtha | 80–500 |
| Aromatics Saturation | 200–500 |
| Isomerization | 50–150 |
| Hydrocracking | 1200–3000 |

Hydrogen consumption at refineries has increased since the 1990s due to increasingly stringent transport fuel requirements, such as lower sulfur standards. Reducing the sulfur content of gasoline requires a deeper level of hydrotreating, which uses more hydrogen per barrel of feed.

[1] "Handbook of Petroleum Refining Processes", 4th Ed. Meyers, Chapter 5.2

